---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Screenshot from "../components/Screenshot.astro";
import UiLabel from "../components/UiLabel.astro";

// Issue #1105: the home page. Sync and multi-guardian family collaboration
// lead (docs/product/positioning.md); every factual claim carries a
// `cite:` comment and a ledger entry in site/claims.md, which
// test/site/site_claims_test.dart checks against the repo. Both sharing
// directions get equal weight, and the age line matches PRIVACY.md §5.

const tagline =
  "A family cycle tracker built for sharing between the people involved.";

const browserUrl = "https://app.lunarlog.app";
---

<BaseLayout title="lunarlog" description={tagline}>
  <section class="hero">
    <h1 class="hero__title">lunarlog</h1>
    <p class="hero__tagline">{tagline}</p>
    <a class="button" href={browserUrl}>Use lunarlog in your browser</a>
    <p class="hero__fineprint">
      Free for personal use. No ads, no data brokers, no tracking networks.
    </p>
  </section>

  <figure class="screenshot-figure screenshot-figure--hero">
    <Screenshot
      screen="today"
      device="iphone-67"
      theme="light"
      loading="eager"
      alt="The lunarlog Today screen: cycle day, flow logged, and the estimate for the next period with its confidence tier."
    />
    <figcaption>
      The Today screen. The estimate is computed on the device from the
      history logged on its profile.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section class="home-section">
    <h2>Built for sharing, not just tracking</h2>
    <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §1 "Built For Sync & Family Collaboration"; docs/product/positioning.md "The family-collaboration story". -->
    <p>
      One account keeps several profiles, and each profile can be shared
      with the people who help look after it. A profile syncs across a
      guardian's own devices, and every entry carries the name of the person
      who logged it. When two guardians log the same day without knowing,
      lunarlog merges the entries instead of overwriting one person's
      writing, and shows both of them what happened.
    </p>
    <!-- cite: role names from lib/l10n/app_en.arb via UiLabel; capability facts from lib/domain/models/profile_guardian.dart. -->
    <p>
      Sharing runs on four roles —
      <UiLabel key="guardianRoleLabelPrimaryGuardian" />,
      <UiLabel key="guardianRoleLabelCoParent" />,
      <UiLabel key="guardianRoleLabelCaregiver" />, and
      <UiLabel key="guardianRoleLabelViewer" /> — so the person you invite
      gets exactly as much access as they need, enforced by the server, not
      just the app. <a href="/family-sharing">How sharing and roles work</a>.
    </p>

    <div class="direction-grid">
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §5 "Her Own Profile (subject membership)"; docs/product/positioning.md "Either side can start". -->
      <div class="direction-card">
        <h3>You track for yourself</h3>
        <p>
          Start on your own device — no account needed to log. When you want
          someone alongside you, invite a parent, partner, or caregiver to
          your profile, as a reader or with hands-on access. You decide the
          role, and you can revoke it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §5 "Her Own Profile" and "Minimum-Age Policy"; issue #957. -->
      <div class="direction-card">
        <h3>You set it up for someone else</h3>
        <p>
          Create a profile for a family member and invite them to log it
          themselves from their own device. For someone under 13, that
          invitation is the only way the app begins — and accepting it is
          the record of parental consent.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      Neither direction is the "real" one, and a parent holding the phone is
      never assumed to be the person whose cycle it is.
      <a href="/family-sharing">Both invitations, step by step</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="home-section">
    <h2>Works when the network doesn't</h2>
    <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §1 "Resilient Offline". -->
    <p>
      Logging, history, and estimates all run from the copy on the device —
      nothing you do offline is blocked or degraded, and what you log syncs
      to the profile's guardians when a connection returns.
      <a href="/tracking">What tracking looks like</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="home-section">
    <h2>Your data, in and out</h2>
    <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §7 (exports, built on-device, no account required); PRIVACY.md §2.A and §4 (Clue, Apple Health, Health Connect import provenance); lib/data/import/clue_importer.dart; lib/data/health/health_import_service.dart. -->
    <p>
      Bring history in from Clue, Apple Health, or Health Connect — always
      started by you, and never over a value you logged by hand. Take
      everything out, any time, as JSON, CSV, a FHIR summary for clinicians,
      or a one-page PDF: built on your device, with no account required.
      <a href="/import">Importing</a> ·
      <a href="/export">Exporting</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="home-section">
    <h2>Estimates, honestly</h2>
    <!-- cite: the app's own framing, lib/domain/help/help_cards.dart ("Estimates only — not medical advice."); confidence tiers in lib/l10n/app_en.arb (cycleConfidence*). -->
    <p>
      Predictions come from the history a profile holds — nothing else — and
      always arrive with a confidence tier, so a fresh profile says so
      instead of guessing. They are estimates only, not medical advice. If
      something worries you, talk to a clinician.
      <a href="/life-stage-modes">How modes change what is computed</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="home-section">
    <h2>Private by design</h2>
    <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §1 (no advertising or data brokers; scrubbed telemetry), §6 (device protection; sync is not end-to-end), §5 (minimum-age policy). -->
    <p>
      No advertising, no data brokers, no behavioral tracking. Your device
      copy sits behind its own lock. Sync runs over TLS into row-level-
      secured storage — and it is not end-to-end encrypted, which the
      privacy page states plainly along with everything else we know about
      your data. lunarlog is licensed 13 and up; someone younger uses it
      only through a parent's invitation.
      <a href="/privacy-security">Privacy and security</a> ·
      <a href="/privacy">The full policy</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <nav class="feature-grid" aria-label="Explore lunarlog">
    <a class="feature-card" href="/tracking">
      <h3>Tracking and viewing</h3>
      <p>Logging a day, reading the calendar, and what the estimates mean.</p>
    </a>
    <a class="feature-card" href="/family-sharing">
      <h3>Family sharing and roles</h3>
      <p>Invitations in both directions, and what each role can do.</p>
    </a>
    <a class="feature-card" href="/life-stage-modes">
      <h3>Life-stage modes</h3>
      <p>From first periods to postpartum — the app adapts, your data stays.</p>
    </a>
    <a class="feature-card" href="/import">
      <h3>Import</h3>
      <p>Bring history in from Clue, Apple Health, or Health Connect.</p>
    </a>
    <a class="feature-card" href="/export">
      <h3>Export</h3>
      <p>JSON, CSV, FHIR, and a clinician PDF — built on your device.</p>
    </a>
    <a class="feature-card" href="/privacy-security">
      <h3>Privacy and security</h3>
      <p>What is protected, what is shared, and what is honest about sync.</p>
    </a>
  </nav>

  <p class="doc__footer-note">
    The full privacy policy lives at <a href="/privacy">lunarlog.app/privacy</a>.
    The app's own Privacy screen summarizes it and links here.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
